          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            In
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+physics"} quantum physics
            the
            %em no-cloning theorem
            is the statement that there cannot be physical processes which produce “copies” of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+states"} quantum states
            in the way known from
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+physics"} classical physics
          %p
            Quantum theoretically, this effect is ultimately due to the non-
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cartesian+monoidal+category"} cartesian
            nature of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensor+product"} tensor product
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensor+product+of+vector+spaces"} of vector
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensor+product+of+Hilbert+spaces"} Hilbert spaces
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/space+of+quantum+states"} of quantum states
            (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+theory"} category theoretically
            it refers to this tensor product lacking
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+transformation"} natural
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/diagonal+morphisms"} diagonal morphisms
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+entanglement"} quantum entanglement
          %p
            In other words when
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+physics"} quantum physics
            is axiomatized by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+logic"} quantum logic
            in the guise of
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+logic"} linear logic
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+type+theory"} linear type theory
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "linear+logic#AsAQuantumLogic"} there
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/diagonal"} diagonal
            map and of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/projection"} projection
            map for the non-
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cartesian+monoidal+category"} cartesian
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensor+product"} tensor product
            in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categorical+semantics"} categorical semantics
            given by non-
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cartesian+monoidal+category"} cartesian
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetric+monoidal+categories"} symmetric monoidal categories
            such as that of Hilbert spaces. See also at
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+information+theory+via+dagger-compact+categories"} quantum information theory via dagger-compact categories
          %h2#details Details
          %h4#ElementaryExplanation Elementary explanation
          %p
            In its original formulation, the statement of the “no-cloning theorem” is that given a quantum system with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hilbert+space"} Hilbert space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi H
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H
            and with a chosen initial pure
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+state"} quantum state
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi e
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi H
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} e \in H
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/unitary+operator"} unitary operator
            on the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensor+product"} tensor product
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi H
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi H
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H \otimes H
            which would take states of the form
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ψ
                  %mo ,
                  %mi e
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\psi, e)
            to
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi ψ
                    %mo ,
                    %mi ψ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\psi,\psi)
          %p
            Of course this cannot exist, because such a map would not even be
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+map"} linear
          %p
            Often the statement is relaxed to observing that if there is a unitary that takes
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ψ
                  %mo ,
                  %mi e
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\psi,e)
            to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ψ
                  %mo ,
                  %mi ψ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\psi,\psi)
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ϕ
                  %mo ,
                  %mi e
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\phi,e)
            to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ϕ
                  %mo ,
                  %mi ϕ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\phi,\phi)
            for any two states
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mo ,
                    %mi ψ
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi H
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi,\psi \in H
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ϕ
                  %mo =
                  %mi ψ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi = \psi
            or
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ϕ
                  %mo ⊥
                  %mi ψ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi \perp \psi
            (since it follows from unitarity that in this case
            = succeed ")." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mi ψ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mi ψ
                    %msup
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \langle\phi | \psi \rangle  = \langle\phi | \psi \rangle^2
          %h4#category_theoretic_explanation Category theoretic explanation
          %p
            More
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+theory"} category theoretically
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensor+product+of+vector+spaces"} tensor product of vector spaces
            (and similarly its refinement
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensor+product+of+Hilbert+spaces"} to Hilbert spaces
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/VectorSpaces"} VectorSpaces
            a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetric+monoidal+category"} symmetric
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+category"} monoidal category
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi Vect
                  %mo ×
                  %mi Vect
                  %mo ⟶
                  %mi Vect
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  (-) \otimes (-)
                  \\;\colon\;
                  Vect \times Vect
                  \\longrightarrow
                  Vect
          %p
            does not admit a
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+transformation"} natural
            transformation of the form
          #eq:WouldBeDiagonal.maruku-equation
            %span.maruku-eq-number (1)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mrow
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi A
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  A \xrightarrow{\;\;} A \otimes A
          %p
            satisfying basic properties expected of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/diagonal+morphisms"} diagonal morphisms
            in a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cartesian+monoidal+category"} cartesian monoidal category
          %p
            Without even requiring
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/projection"} projection
            maps and hence “deleting” operations (which are part of a
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cartesian+product"} cartesian product
            %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:WouldBeDiagonal"} (1)
            would have to satisfy are
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coassociativity"} coassociativity
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cocommutativity"} cocommutativity
            – and one finds that such cannot hold in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/VectorSpaces"} VectorSpaces
            \&amp;lbrack;
            %a{:href => "#Abramsky09"}> Abramsky (2009), Thm. 11
            \&amp;rbrack;.
          %p
          #RelationToQuantumMeasurement.num_remark
            %h6 Remark
            %p
              %strong
                (relation to classical outcomes of
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+measurement"} quantum measurement
              %br/
              Notice that the condition that
              %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:WouldBeDiagonal"} (1)
              be a
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+transformation"} natural transformation
              is crucial for the above conclusion of “no cloning”:
            %p
              Namely, for a
              %em fixed
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vector+space"} vector space
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi.mathscript ℋ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathscr{H}
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+basis"} linear basis
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} {
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mi b
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                    %msub
                      %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} }
                      %mrow
                        %mi b
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                        %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \big\{ \vert b \rangle \big\}_{b \colon B}
              does induce a single
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+map"} linear map
              of the required form, given by duplicating (hence “cloning”) the
              %em fixed basis
              elements:
            #eq:CoalgebraStructureInducedByLinearBasis.maruku-equation
              %span.maruku-eq-number (2)
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi.mathscript ℋ
                          %mtd
                            %mo ⟶
                          %mtd
                            %mi.mathscript ℋ
                            %mo ⊗
                            %mi.mathscript ℋ
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                            %mi b
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                          %mtd
                            %mo ↦
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                            %mi b
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                            %mo ⊗
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                            %mi b
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\array{
                    \\mathscr{H}
                    \&amp;\longrightarrow&amp;
                    \\mathscr{H}
                    \\otimes
                    \\mathscr{H}
                    \\\
                    \\vert b \rangle
                    \&amp;\mapsto&amp;
                    \\vert b \rangle
                    \\otimes
                    \\vert b \rangle
                    }
            %p
              On the other hand, beware that, by the laws of
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+maps"} linear maps
              %em no other
              elements except the basis elements are exactly cloned even by this operation; for example the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/superposition"} superposition
              of two distinct basis elements goes to:
            #eq:ExampleOfNonCloningOfCoalgebraMap.maruku-equation
              %span.maruku-eq-number (3)
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mtable{:columnalign => "left center left", :displaystyle => "false", :rowspacing => "0.5ex"}
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                          %mi b
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                          %mo +
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                          %mi b
                          %mo ′
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                        %mtd
                          %mo ↦
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                          %mi b
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                          %mo ⊗
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                          %mi b
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                          %mo +
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                          %mi b
                          %mo ′
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                          %mo ⊗
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                          %mi b
                          %mo ′
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo =
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} (
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                          %mi b
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                          %mo +
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                          %mi b
                          %mo ′
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                          %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} )
                          %mo ⊗
                          %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} (
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                          %mi b
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                          %mo +
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                          %mi b
                          %mo ′
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                          %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} )
                          %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                          %mo −
                          %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                          %munder
                            %munder
                              %mrow
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                                %mi b
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                                %mo ⊗
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                                %mi b
                                %mo ′
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                                %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                                %mo +
                                %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                                %mi b
                                %mo ′
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                                %mo ⊗
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                                %mi b
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                              %mo ⏟
                            %mi correction
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\begin{array}{lcl}
                    \\vert b \rangle + \vert b' \rangle
                    \&amp;\mapsto&amp;
                    \\vert b \rangle \otimes \vert b \rangle
                    +
                    \\vert b' \rangle \otimes \vert b' \rangle
                    \\\
                    \&amp;=&amp;
                    \\big(
                    \\vert b \rangle + \vert b' \rangle
                    \\big)
                    \\otimes
                    \\big(
                    \\vert b \rangle + \vert b' \rangle
                    \\big)
                    \\,-\,
                    \\underset{correction}{
                    \\underbrace{
                    \\vert b \rangle \otimes \vert b' \rangle
                    \\,+\,
                    \\vert b' \rangle \otimes \vert b \rangle
                    }
                    }
                    \\end{array}
            %p
              Nonetheless, the linear map
              %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:CoalgebraStructureInducedByLinearBasis"} (2)
              does satisfy
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coassociativity"} coassociativity
              and
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cocommutativity"} cocommutativity
              in the evident sense; in fact it also satisfies
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/counitality"} counitality
              with respect to the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/unit"} unit
              element
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mn 1
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mo ≔
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %munder
                        %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                        %mrow
                          %mi b
                          %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                          %mi B
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                      %mi b
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mo ∈
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mi.mathscript ℋ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1 \,\coloneqq\, \underset{b \colon B}{\sum} \vert b \rangle \;\in\; \mathscr{H}
              %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:CoalgebraStructureInducedByLinearBasis"} (2)
              defines a
              = succeed "-" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cocommutative+coalgebra"} cocommutative coalgebra
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/structure"} structure
              on
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi.mathscript ℋ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathscr{H}
              %em any
              (
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite-dimensional+vector+space"}> finite-dimensional
              )
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/space+of+quantum+states"} space of quantum states
            %p
              While such structures exist, they do not represent a
              %em general
              process of “cloning” of quantum states: Due to effects as in
              = succeed "," do
                %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:ExampleOfNonCloningOfCoalgebraMap"} (3)
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+transformations"} natural transformations
              and hence they “clone” not arbitrary quantum states, but just the chosen
              = succeed "-states" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+basis"} linear basis
            %p
              Conversely, such
              = succeed "-" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coalgebra"} coalgebra
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/structure"} structure
              (at least when regarded as the special symmetric
              = succeed "-" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Frobenius+algebra"} Frobenius algebra
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/structure"} structure
              to which they always extend) in fact
              %em define
              a choice of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+basis"} linear basis
              of a quantum space of states. Since such a choice may encode the set of possible classical state outcomes under a
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+measurement"} quantum measurement
              = succeed "\u201D" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+structures"} classical structures
              = succeed ")." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+information+theory+in+terms+of+dagger-compact+categories"} quantum information theory in terms of dagger-compact categories
            %p
              In summary: While a
              %em natural
              cloning operation on quantum states does not exist,
              %em enforcing
              one on a select
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+basis"} linear basis
              of quantum states is tantamount to “turning these into classical states” (in a sense that can be made more precise, see discussion at
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+reader+monad"} quantum reader monad
              and
              = succeed ")." do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+circuits+via+dependent+linear+types"} quantum circuits via dependent linear types
            %p
          %p
          .num_remark
            %h6 Remark
            %p
              %strong (Converse statement)
              %br/
              One may turn the issue around asks which conditions are imposed on a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetric+monoidal+category"} symmetric monoidal category
              if its
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensor+product"} tensor product
              is assumed to admit a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/diagonal+map"} diagonal map
              (for “cloning”) or
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/projection"} projection
              maps (for “deleting”) or both. These turn out to be very strong conditions, asserting that the category must be “essentially classical”:
            %p
              For instance in order for a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/diagonal"} diagonal
              to exist for the tensor product of a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/compact+closed+category"} compact closed category
              implies that every
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/endomorphism"} endomorphism
              on every
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/object"} object
              in the category is a multiple of the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/identity"} identity
              \&amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "#Abramsky09"} Abramsky (2009), Thm. 11
              in generalization of “
              %a{:href => "dualizing+object+in+a+closed+category#InACartesianClosedCategory"}> Joyal’s lemma
              ”&amp;rbrack;. This is clearly false in any category in which one could find interesting
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+mechanics"} quantum mechanics
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hilbert+spaces"} Hilbert spaces
          %h3#no_broadcasting No broadcasting
          %p
            There is a slightly more substantial generalization of the no-cloning theorem to
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mixed+quantum+states"} mixed quantum states
            = succeed "." do
              %em no-broadcasting theorem
            = succeed "." do
              %em no-deleting theorem
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/deferred+measurement+principle"} deferred measurement principle
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+superposition"} quantum superposition
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/entanglement"} entanglement
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bell%27s+inequalities"} Bell's inequalities
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gleason%27s+theorem"} Gleason's theorem
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/interpretation+of+quantum+mechanics"} interpretation of quantum mechanics
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+cryptography"} quantum cryptography
          %h2#references References
          %h3#nocloning No-cloning
          %p The original articles on the “no-cloning theorem”:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dennis+Dieks"}> Dennis Dieks
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Communication in EPR devices
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/William+Wooters"}> William Wooters
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Wojciech+Zurek"} Wojciech Zurek
                = succeed "," do
                  %em A single quantum cannot be cloned
                %strong 299
                (1982) 802-803 &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1038/299802a0"}> doi:10.1038/299802a0
                \&amp;rbrack;
          %p See also:
          %ul
            %li
              Wikipedia,
              %em
                %a{:href => "http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/No-cloning_theorem"} No-cloning theorem
          %p
            Discussion from the point of view of
            = succeed "-theory" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+category"} monoidal category
            = succeed "):" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+information+theory+via+dagger-compact+categories"} quantum information theory via dagger-compact categories
          %ul
            %li#Abramsky09
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Samson+Abramsky"}> Samson Abramsky
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em No-Cloning in categorical quantum mechanics
              = succeed "," do
                %em Semantic Techniques for Quantum Computation
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/0910.2401"} arXiv:0910.2401
              = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1017/CBO9781139193313.002"} doi:10.1017/CBO9781139193313.002
          %p
            The original suggestion to use the no-cloning theorem for
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+cryptography"} quantum cryptography
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Stephen+Wiesner"}> Stephen Wiesner
              ,
              %em Conjugate Coding
              circulated in the 1960s, finally published in ACM SIGACT News
              %strong 15
              1 (1083) 78–88 (
              %a{:href => "http://users.cms.caltech.edu/~vidick/teaching/120_qcrypto/wiesner.pdf"}> original pdf
              ,
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1145/1008908.1008920"} doi:10.1145/1008908.1008920
          %h3#nodeleting No-deleting
          %p The original statement of the “no-deleting theorem”:
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Arun+Kumar+Pati"}> Arun Kumar Pati
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Samuel+L.+Braunstein"} Samuel L. Braunstein
              = succeed "," do
                %em Impossibility of deleting an unknown quantum state
              %strong 404
              (2000) 164-165 &amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1038/404130b0"}> doi:10.1038/404130b0
              ,
              = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/quant-ph/9911090"} arXiv:quant-ph/9911090
          %p See also:
          %ul
            %li
              Wikipedia,
              %em
                %a{:href => "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/No-deleting_theorem"} No-deleting theorem
          %p
            Discussion via
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+information+theory+via+dagger-compact+categories"} quantum information theory via dagger-compact categories
          %ul
            %li
